/**
  @page guides-weak-formulations Weak formulations
  @brief Derive a variational equation before translating it into finite element code.

  A weak formulation specifies the spaces in which an equation is tested, the
  derivatives that must exist, and the way boundary data enter. The purpose of
  this guide is the derivation. The @ref guides-variational "variational formulations
  guide" then explains how those objects are represented in Rodin.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-weak-poisson Begin with scalar diffusion

  Let @f$\Omega\subset\mathbb R^d@f$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain. Split its
  boundary into a Dirichlet part @f$\Gamma_D@f$ and a Neumann part
  @f$\Gamma_N@f$, ignoring their measure-zero interface. Consider
  @f[
    -\Delta u=f\quad\text{in }\Omega,\qquad
    u=g_D\quad\text{on }\Gamma_D,\qquad
    \nabla u\cdot n=g_N\quad\text{on }\Gamma_N.
  @f]
  For nonzero prescribed values, the admissible fields and test functions belong
  to different sets:
  @f[
    U=\{w\in H^1(\Omega):w|_{\Gamma_D}=g_D\},\qquad
    V=\{v\in H^1(\Omega):v|_{\Gamma_D}=0\}.
  @f]
  Traces are understood in the Sobolev sense. The data must admit the required
  trace and load functionals. In particular, a lifting of @f$g_D@f$ is assumed
  to exist in @f$H^1(\Omega)@f$.

  @subsection guides-weak-poisson-deriv Integrate by parts

  Multiplying by @f$v\in V@f$ and integrating by parts gives
  @f[
    \int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dx
       -\int_{\partial\Omega}(\nabla u\cdot n)v\,ds
       =\int_\Omega fv\,dx.
  @f]
  The test function vanishes on @f$\Gamma_D@f$, while the normal derivative is
  prescribed on @f$\Gamma_N@f$. Thus the weak problem is: find @f$u\in U@f$
  such that
  @f[
    a(u,v)=\ell(v)\quad\text{for every }v\in V,
  @f]
  with
  @f[
    a(u,v)=\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dx,\qquad
    \ell(v)=\int_\Omega fv\,dx+\int_{\Gamma_N}g_Nv\,ds.
  @f]
  The second derivative in the strong equation has become first derivatives
  in the weak equation. This permits continuous piecewise affine functions,
  whose gradients may jump between cells, to be used as conforming approximations.

  @subsection guides-weak-poisson-result Check uniqueness

  On a connected domain with a Dirichlet part of positive boundary measure,
  the Poincaré inequality supplies coercivity of the diffusion form on @f$V@f$.
  With a bounded load functional, the standard Lax–Milgram argument gives a
  unique weak solution after lifting the prescribed values.

  For a pure-Neumann problem, constants remain in the kernel. Compatibility
  requires
  @f[
    \int_\Omega f\,dx+\int_{\partial\Omega}g_N\,ds=0.
  @f]
  A mean constraint or another valid normalization is then needed. A solver
  cannot compensate for incompatible data or an unresolved nullspace.

  @subsection guides-weak-poisson-rodin Connect the terms to code

  The @ref guides-boundary-conditions "mixed-boundary example" takes
  @f$f=0@f$, fixes the left side of the unit square, and prescribes a unit
  normal derivative on the right:

  @snippet PDEs/MixedBoundary.cpp mixed-boundary-form

  The minus sign on the load expresses @f$a(u,v)-\ell(v)=0@f$.
  Boundary selection and the complete runnable program are explained in that
  guide. For zero values on the entire boundary and a nonzero source, see the
  @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial".

  @section guides-weak-elasticity Extend to elasticity

  The unknown displacement is @f$u:\Omega\to\mathbb R^d@f$. Define
  @f[
    \varepsilon(u)=\tfrac12(\nabla u+\nabla u^T),\qquad
    \sigma(u)=\lambda\operatorname{div}(u)I+2\mu\varepsilon(u).
  @f]
  Equilibrium is @f$-\operatorname{div}\sigma(u)=b@f$, with prescribed
  displacement on @f$\Gamma_D@f$ and traction @f$\sigma(u)n=t@f$ on
  @f$\Gamma_N@f$. Testing against a vector field that vanishes on the fixed
  boundary gives
  @f[
    \int_\Omega\sigma(u):\varepsilon(v)\,dx
       =\int_\Omega b\cdot v\,dx+\int_{\Gamma_N}t\cdot v\,ds.
  @f]
  Symmetry of the stress removes its contraction with the skew part of
  @f$\nabla v@f$. Expanding the constitutive law gives
  @f[
    a(u,v)=\int_\Omega
      \left[\lambda\operatorname{div}(u)\operatorname{div}(v)
           +2\mu\varepsilon(u):\varepsilon(v)\right]dx.
  @f]
  The factor @f$2\mu@f$ is essential with this definition of strain.

  @subsection guides-weak-elasticity-lame Interpret the material model

  Positive elastic energy requires @f$\mu>0@f$ and
  @f$\lambda+2\mu/d>0@f$. The displacement constraints must also remove rigid
  motions; Korn's inequality then supplies the relevant coercivity statement.

  For three-dimensional isotropic elasticity, Young's modulus @f$E@f$ and
  Poisson's ratio @f$\nu@f$ give
  @f[
    \mu=\frac{E}{2(1+\nu)},\qquad
    \lambda=\frac{E\nu}{(1+\nu)(1-2\nu)}.
  @f]
  A two-dimensional reduction must state whether plane strain or plane stress
  is intended. The @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "beam tutorial" uses plane
  strain and shows the corresponding vector-valued finite element problem.

  @subsection guides-weak-elasticity-hyperelastic Finite-strain models

  Linearized strain assumes small deformation. Finite-strain mechanics
  uses the deformation gradient and a constitutive energy, from which an
  internal virtual-work residual and consistent tangent are obtained. The
  @ref guides-solid-mechanics "solid mechanics guide" explains that construction;
  the residual and its consistent tangent must represent the chosen nonlinear model.

  @section guides-weak-complex Complex and mixed formulations

  Over complex fields, the usual finite element convention is trial-linear
  and test-conjugate-linear. For Helmholtz, this gives
  @f[
    a(u,v)=\int_\Omega\nabla u\cdot\overline{\nabla v}\,dx
               -k^2\int_\Omega u\overline v\,dx.
  @f]
  Positive definiteness is checked from the coefficients, boundary conditions and
  spectrum of the form. The
  @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "Helmholtz tutorial" uses an indefinite system
  and checks a manufactured solution.

  Mixed equations introduce coupled unknowns and constraints. In @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes flow", velocity and
  pressure form a saddle-point system; compatible spaces and a pressure gauge
  are needed. In @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes "Navier–Stokes", convection
  adds a nonlinear dependence that must be discretized in time and linearized
  explicitly.

  @section guides-weak-galerkin Discretize the weak problem

  Choose a finite-dimensional test space @f$V_h\subset V@f$ and a discrete
  representation of the prescribed trace. For homogeneous essential data,
  trial and test spaces can coincide. With trial basis functions @f$\phi_j@f$ and test basis functions @f$\psi_i@f$,
  the Galerkin equations have entries
  @f[
    A_{ij}=a(\phi_j,\psi_i),\qquad b_i=\ell(\psi_i).
  @f]
  For a nonzero discrete lifting @f$g_h@f$, the unknown homogeneous part instead
  has load entries @f$\ell(\psi_i)-a(g_h,\psi_i)@f$. If the prescribed trace
  is not exactly representable, its approximation introduces a boundary-data
  error. Periodic identifications or multiplier equations add their corresponding
  algebraic constraints. Numerical quadrature and approximated geometry can
  introduce additional errors beyond the field approximation.

  A small algebraic residual establishes that the assembled system was solved
  accurately. Model accuracy and spatial discretization error are assessed separately. See the @ref guides-variational "form-language guide" for
  assembly, @ref guides-solvers "solvers" for algebraic checks, and
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts "numerical contracts" for implementation rules.
*/
